    body: `Elvis Eghosa Ogiekpolor lived in Norcross, Georgia, when federal prosecutors say he built one of the more elaborate money-laundering operations to come out of the wave of online romance and business email compromise fraud that swept the United States in the late 2010s. Between October 2018 and August 2020, Ogiekpolor opened, and directed others to open, more than 50 fraudulent business bank accounts under roughly a dozen shell companies — accounts that existed for one purpose: to receive money that other scammers had already talked victims out of.

The romance-fraud side of the operation followed a familiar pattern that, at scale, proved devastating. Scammers cultivated relationships with victims on dating sites and social media, invented reasons they needed money urgently, and then directed the funds into Ogiekpolor's network of accounts rather than back to any real business. At his eight-day federal trial, thirteen romance-fraud victims testified about the toll of the deception, including one woman who described sending nearly $70,000 to a man she had met on eHarmony and believed she was in a relationship with. Alongside the romance scams, Ogiekpolor's network also processed proceeds from business email compromise schemes, in which fraudsters impersonate executives or vendors to trick companies into wiring payments to accounts they control. Once the money landed in one of his fraudulent accounts, Ogiekpolor moved it onward through a further layer of domestic and overseas accounts, obscuring its origin before it could be traced back to the original victims.

Investigators ultimately linked more than $9.5 million in fraud proceeds to the accounts Ogiekpolor controlled or directed. Five co-conspirators — Vere Whyte, Kutina Crawford, Ebony Parks, Tiffany Gaines, and Tamara Gaines — pleaded guilty to their roles in the scheme, while Ogiekpolor himself went to trial, where six cooperating money mules testified against him in addition to the thirteen romance-fraud victims. A federal jury convicted him of money laundering and conspiracy to commit money laundering.

In October 2022, a federal judge sentenced Ogiekpolor to 25 years in federal prison followed by three years of supervised release — a sentence prosecutors and the FBI described as reflecting both the scale of the losses and the deliberate structure he built to move the money. "Ogiekpolor and his co-conspirators were part of a broader international network of online fraudsters and money launderers who wreaked havoc and devastation on unsuspecting individuals and businesses," the U.S. Attorney's office said in announcing the sentence, while an FBI Atlanta official noted that no sentence could make the victims whole again, only hold the people responsible accountable.

The case illustrates the division of labor that has come to define large-scale romance and business-email-compromise fraud: the people who write the fake love letters and impersonate executives are rarely the same people who spend fifty bank accounts' worth of effort making the stolen money disappear, and it is often the laundering network — not any single scammer — that determines how much money a fraud ring can extract before it gets caught. Ogiekpolor's 25-year sentence, among the longest ever handed down in a U.S. romance-scam money-laundering case, reflected a prosecution strategy aimed squarely at that layer of the operation.`,
