    body: `Eijroghene Okuma — identified in Securities and Exchange Commission filings by the fuller name Ejiro Ode Okuma — spent more than a decade as a financial and investment advisor with Edward Jones, working in the Atlanta area, before federal prosecutors say he turned a position of complete trust into a multimillion-dollar theft from a single elderly client. Okuma, 43, of Lithia Springs, Georgia, had full access to the Edward Jones brokerage accounts of a client born in 1944, a relationship built over years of ordinary retirement-planning work that gave no outward sign of what was coming.

The scheme began, according to the Justice Department and the SEC, in February 2022, when Okuma was appointed to administer the estate of the client's recently deceased sister — a role that gave him a plausible-sounding reason to move money around on the client's behalf. Beginning the following month, prosecutors said, Okuma started embezzling from the client by falsely claiming the sister's estate needed funds, then escalated: he opened unauthorized accounts in the client's name, transferred approximately $9 million in securities into an account he personally controlled, created fake login credentials and an email address impersonating the client to keep the fraud from surfacing, and forged signatures and fraudulent checks to move money into accounts tied to his own family. By the time the scheme was uncovered, the SEC's parallel civil complaint said, Okuma had misappropriated more than $9.8 million.

None of the stolen money went toward the client's benefit. Okuma used it to build a $5.2 million residence in the affluent Atlanta suburb of Vinings, put roughly $1.4 million toward a fractional share of a beach house and beach-club membership, and donate approximately $340,000 to his church, according to federal prosecutors.

Okuma pleaded guilty in March 2026 to one count of wire fraud, which carries a statutory maximum of 20 years in prison. "This was the ultimate act of financial betrayal," said U.S. Attorney Theodore S. Hertzberg of the Northern District of Georgia in announcing the plea. "Instead of abiding by his fiduciary obligations, Okuma exploited an elderly man's trust and stole millions." Separately, in a settled civil action the SEC had already resolved two months earlier, in January 2026, Okuma agreed to a judgment requiring him to pay $9,025,424.89 in disgorgement, $1,029,626.64 in prejudgment interest, and a $3 million civil penalty — more than $13 million in total — and to a permanent injunction against future securities-law violations. Okuma's criminal sentencing was calendared for June 23, 2026, before U.S. District Judge Sarah E. Geraghty; publicly available reporting reviewed for this profile did not include the outcome of that hearing, leaving the length of his eventual prison term unconfirmed as of this writing.

Okuma's case illustrates how a fiduciary role built over a decade of ordinary, unremarkable service can become the very cover a theft needs: the "estate administration" story he used to justify the first transfers was plausible precisely because it fit the kind of task a trusted advisor legitimately handles, and the forged logins and impersonated emails he built afterward existed only to keep the client from ever comparing notes with the institution actually holding the money. It is a reminder that even a large, well-known brokerage relationship offers no independent verification of an individual advisor's own separate transfers, accounts, or correspondence — that still requires a client, or a family member acting on their behalf, to check directly with the firm rather than trusting an advisor's account of what is happening to money once it leaves the original custodian.`,
