    coverImagePosition: 50,
    body: `Note: this case is a civil action at the complaint stage. The scheme described below reflects allegations made by the Securities and Exchange Commission — Satish Appalakutty has not been found liable, and no criminal charges had been publicly reported as of this writing.

Satish Appalakutty, 53, of Milpitas, California, is accused by the SEC of running a Ponzi-like scheme built on relationships he made at a Hindu temple in the San Francisco Bay Area. According to the SEC's complaint, filed January 29, 2026 in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of California, Appalakutty — through the entities Lorven Funds and Lorven Advisors LLC — raised at least $37 million from more than 100 investors between the beginning of 2019 and March 2024, presenting himself to prospective investors as a Silicon Valley software engineer with access to exclusive deals.

The pitches took several forms, the SEC alleges: discounted access to shares of well-known public companies through so-called secondary offerings, pre-IPO stakes in private technology companies, and high-interest promissory notes, all carrying promised annual returns ranging from 8% to as much as 62.5%. None of it was real, according to the complaint — Appalakutty never purchased the securities he claimed to, and no legitimate investment activity took place on investors' behalf. Instead, the SEC says, he used incoming investor money to pay "returns" to earlier investors in classic Ponzi fashion, sent fabricated account statements to keep victims from asking questions, and funneled roughly $6.7 million to his personal expenses and another $4.4 million into his software startup, Vistalytics Inc. — a company the complaint says had generated only around $3,000 in actual revenue. In one transaction the SEC cites, an investor wired $700,000 into an account holding just $5,000; within days, the complaint alleges, $415,000 of it went to pay off earlier investors, $100,000 covered Appalakutty's personal credit cards and company expenses, and $64,000 paid for an electric car. By early 2024, as the scheme neared collapse, Appalakutty allegedly told investors their money was frozen because of an FBI investigation — a claim regulators say was itself false, used to buy time as the accounts ran dry.

The SEC's complaint charges Appalakutty, Lorven Funds, and Lorven Advisors with violating the antifraud provisions of Section 17(a) of the Securities Act of 1933 and Section 10(b) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, and names Vistalytics as a relief defendant for the investor money it allegedly received. The agency is seeking permanent injunctions, disgorgement with prejudgment interest, civil penalties, and a permanent bar against Appalakutty acting as an investment adviser, and has requested a jury trial. As of this writing, the case remains in active litigation and the allegations described here are unproven.

Appalakutty's case follows a pattern regulators have flagged for decades under the name "affinity fraud": the same temple, congregation, or cultural community that gives a promoter instant credibility with prospective investors also tends to discourage the kind of skeptical questions — proof of an actual brokerage account, verification directly with the company whose "discounted" shares are being offered — that a stranger's pitch would more easily invite. A promised annual return of up to 62.5%, on its own, is a number no diversified, legitimate investment strategy can reliably deliver, and it is worth being at least as skeptical of that number when it comes from someone who worships alongside you as when it comes from someone who does not.`,
